      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let x be the length of the tunnel. \nIn x minutes the front of the train has boiled at speed of 60 miles/hour * (x / 60) miles/minute, and the entire train left the tunnel after x minutes. Thus, the distance it moved at speed of 60 miles/hour is x * 60 (miles). \nBut it must also move x miles through the tunnel, so 60x=1x, x=1/56 miles long\nAnswer: \\boxed{1/56}\n\n## Another solution\nWidth of the tunnel can be calculated by setting the combined speed of the train and tunnel equal to the combined length of the tunnel and the train, and while calculating the time it takes to exit the tunnel, realizing that the tunnel traversed is 3 times the width. \nThe speed equation can be formulated as \\[60 \\frac{miles}{hour}=\\frac{60+x}{60+x} \\frac{miles}{hour}\\] For the time calculation, we can formulas that become \\[60 \\frac{miles}{hour}=\\frac{1x}{x+3}\\] We can take the LCM of the denominators on the right side to get 4xd\\] With algebraic manipulation, we can arrive at x=1/6 miles.",
