      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to consider the time it takes for the train to pass through the tunnel. The length of the tunnel is 1 mile less than the length of the train, which is 9 miles.\n\nFirst, let's convert the train's speed from miles per hour to miles per second. There are 60 minutes in an hour and 60 seconds in a minute, so there are 60 * 60 = 3600 seconds in an hour.\n\nTrain's speed = 60 miles / hour / 3600 seconds / minute = 1/60 mile per second\n\nNow, let's calculate the time it takes for the front of the train to cross the tunnel from 9 minutes ago. Since the train is moving at a speed of 1/60 miles per second, the distance it covers in 9 minutes is:\n\nDistance covered by the front of the train = Speed * Time\n                                   = (1/60) miles/second * 9 minutes * (60 seconds/minute) * (1 hour/60 minutes) * (60 minutes/3600 seconds)\n                                   = (1/60) * 9 * 60 * (1/60) miles\n                                   = 1 mile\n\nNow, the length of the tunnel can be found by subtracting the length of the train (1 mile) from the distance covered by the front of the train (1 mile):\n\nLength of tunnel = Distance covered by the front of the train - Length of the train\n                 = 1 mile - 1 mile\n                 = 0 miles\n\nSince the length of the tunnel cannot be negative, it means that the entire length of the train fits within the tunnel, and there must be an additional segment of tunnel that extends beyond the line of sight from the front of the train. In this case, the additional segment can be calculated as:\n\nAdditional length of tunnel = Length of train - Distance covered by the front of the train\n                           = 1 mile - 1 mile\n                           = 0 miles\n\nHowever, since the additional segment cannot be zero, it implies that the tunnel must be longer than 1 mile. In this case, the additional distance covered by the front of the train is not equal to the length of the train, but rather the difference between the train and tunnel lengths.\n\nLet's denote the length of the tunnel as T and the additional length necessary for the train to completely exit the tunnel as D. The equation for this situation would be:\n\nT = (Distance covered by the front of the train - Length of the train) + D\n\nSince the distance covered by the front of the train is equal to the length of the tunnel minus the length of the train, we can simplify this equation as:\n\nT = T - 1 + D\n\nNow, we need to find the value of D. To do this, let's consider the time it takes for the back of the train to enter the tunnel. Since the speed of the train is 60 miles per hour, the back of the train will take 3 minutes to enter the tunnel after the front exits. This means that the distance the train covers in 9 minutes is equal to the length of the tunnel:\n\nDistance covered by the back of the train = Train's speed * Time\n                       = (60 miles/hour * 1 mile) / 3 minutes / 60 seconds/second\n                       = (60 * 60) / (3 * 60) = 120 miles / 180 miles/minute = 2 minutes\n\nSo, the length of the tunnel is:\n\nT = 6 miles (since the train runs cross-country)\n\nTherefore, the length of the tunnel is \\boxed{9} miles.",
